WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.440
Could a group of cells that completes itself also be set going while the animal

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.440
rests?

3
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:13.570
One proposal: the hippocampus replays experience afterwards, teaching the cortex.

4
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.790
The evidence moves up the lesson's ladder: from correlation to turning ripple

5
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:19.790
activity off.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.540
Matthew Wilson and Bruce McNaughton recorded many hippocampal cells at once in three

7
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.500
rats.

8
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.140
They compared sleep before a spatial task, the task itself, and sleep after it.

9
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:36.203
Pairs that co-fired in the task co-fired more in the sleep after it than before.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:39.973
Pairs that were silent in the task showed no such rise.

11
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:44.703
This was pairwise co-firing, and a correlation, not a test of memory.

12
00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:47.006
Later work read the order.

13
00:00:47.946 --> 00:00:50.746
Each row here is one place cell, sorted along the track.

14
00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:54.749
During a run, the spikes form a diagonal, cell after cell.

15
00:00:55.283 --> 00:01:00.143
In pauses at the end of a run, the sequence replayed in reverse, compressed in time.

16
00:01:00.766 --> 00:01:02.566
Before runs, it played forward.

17
00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:08.719
Much replay rides on sharp-wave ripples, in the local field potential: the summed

18
00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:11.179
electrical signal from cells near an electrode.

19
00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:16.939
A sharp-wave ripple is a slower sharp wave carrying a burst of very fast ripple

20
00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:19.539
waves. It lasts tens of milliseconds.

21
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:25.123
A computer can detect each ripple as it starts, and trigger a stimulus that cuts it

22
00:01:25.123 --> 00:01:26.033
short.

23
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.593
Rats given this in sleep after training do worse next day.

24
00:01:29.593 --> 00:01:33.133
What control do you need before blaming the lost ripples?

25
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:43.743
Give another group the same stimuli, each delayed after a ripple, so its ripples run

26
00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:44.743
their course.

27
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:47.283
Gabrielle Girardeau and colleagues did this.

28
00:01:48.103 --> 00:01:49.783
Only the ripple-cut group was impaired.

29
00:01:50.416 --> 00:01:53.656
So the stimulation alone does not explain the deficit.

30
00:01:54.556 --> 00:01:56.236
Its timing does: hitting the ripples.

31
00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:58.786
Another group went the other way.

32
00:01:59.546 --> 00:02:02.806
Prolonging ripples in awake rats improved maze working memory.

33
00:02:03.416 --> 00:02:06.916
That tested awake ripples, not consolidation during sleep.

34
00:02:07.516 --> 00:02:08.816
One limit remains.

35
00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:13.556
Cutting ripples removes ripple-linked activity in general, not one route.

36
00:02:14.149 --> 00:02:17.729
The ripple evidence climbed from correlation to perturbation.

37
00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:21.569
Ripple-linked activity aids memory, in the tasks tested.
